    <h2>How they shoot differently</h2>
    <p><strong>Marksman Barrel:</strong> Gen5 uses conventional rifling with a recessed crown instead of the older polygonal profile. Glock lists it as improved accuracy and more flexibility with bullet types.</p>
    <p><strong>Flared magwell and front serrations:</strong> The flare helps guide a magazine in. Front serrations give a forward press check option. Gen3 has neither.</p>
    <p><strong>Controls:</strong> Ambi slide stop and reversible release make Gen5 usable left handed without parts. Gen3 requires a right hand release.</p>
    <p><strong>Recoil:</strong> Gen5 dual spring was first introduced in Gen4. It spreads recoil over a longer stroke. Gen3 single spring is harsher on .40, but in 9mm the difference is modest.</p>

    <h2>Which one should you pick in Texas</h2>
    <p><strong>Choose Gen3 if:</strong> you want maximum aftermarket support, you need California compliance history, or you found a used listing at a lower price. This catalogue has used Glock pistols in a separate collection. Gen3 parts are everywhere.</p>
    <p><strong>Choose Gen5 if:</strong> you are buying new, you want the current nDLC finish for sweat and humidity, you want front serrations and an optic ready MOS option, or you shoot left handed. For a new buyer in Texas, Gen5 is the simpler recommendation because it is what Glock currently produces and warranties from Smyrna.</p>
    <p>Texas adds no extra restriction on either generation. Both transfer the same way: ship to your pickup location where the licensed dealer completes ATF Form 4473, run FBI NICS, release on proceed. No state permit, no waiting period.</p>

